{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 15, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "**Summary of the Wikipedia page \"Raymond III Count of Tripoli\"**\n\n**Early life and succession**\nRaymond III Count of Tripoli was born in 1140 as the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Rethel. He became Count of Tripoli in 1152 after his father was assassinated by the Hashashin. His mother Hodierna ruled as regent until he reached adulthood in 1155. Raymond III was a member of the House of Poitiers and a cousin of King Amalric I of Jerusalem.\n\n**Political career and conflicts**\nRaymond III played a significant role in the politics of the Crusader states. He supported his cousin King Amalric I and was involved in the succession crisis after Amalrics death in 1174. Raymond III was a key figure in the coronation of Baldwin IV the Leper as king of Jerusalem. He also served as regent for Baldwin IV from 1174 to 1177 and again from 1183 to 1185. During his regency he faced challenges from factions led by Guy of Lusignan and Raymond of Saint-Gilles.\n\n**Capture and death**\nIn 1187 Raymond III was captured by Saladin after the Battle of Hattin. He was held captive for several years until his release in 1190. Raymond III died in 1187 or possibly later in 1190. His death marked the end of an era in the history of the County of Tripoli and the Crusader states.\n\n**Marriage and legacy**\nRaymond III married Echive of Bures but the couple had no children. As a result his title passed to his cousin Bohemond IV of Antioch. Raymond III is remembered as a skilled diplomat and a capable ruler who navigated the complex politics of the Crusader states during a turbulent period. His actions helped shape the course of events during the late 12th century in the Levant.", "logprobs": [{"token": "**", "logprob": -0.17142000794410706}, {"token": "Summary", "logprob": -1.686187744140625}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.0006282739923335612}, {"